epic         V3
title        Flow State v3 production
status       active
description
    **Goal:** "production code for Flow State v3 at high quality, with good error messages and the
    correctness guarantees the prototypes proved, plus example apps and generated documentation"
    (the user, 2026-11-04). Built with skill:to-epic at the library revision in skill.

    Records: Plans @plan.FlowState, @plan.Machines, @plan.Operations, @plan.Runtime,
    @plan.Persistence, @plan.EntityDomains, @plan.React, @plan.ServerRendering, @plan.Testing,
    @plan.Inspection, @plan.Diagnostics, @plan.CommandLine, @plan.LazyFeatures, @plan.Package
    (merged in phase 2, status proposed).

    Core Specs @spec.Compile, @spec.Turn, @spec.Actors, @spec.WriteLanes, @spec.Reads,
    @spec.WriteAhead, @spec.Restore, @spec.Diagnostics, @spec.Codec, @spec.StaticCli. Deferred and
    open items: code:workflow/plans/flow/references/proposals/revisit.md,
    code:workflow/plans/flow/references/proposals/open-items.md.

    Locations:

    - production code `packages/flow-state-v3/src/` (created in P4);
    - the conformance suite code:packages/flow-state-v3/conformance/README.md (its how-to);
    - the prototype reference `packages/flow-state-v3/prototypes/round-14/` and its old suite
      `prototypes/conformance/`;
    - feature Specs beside the core ones in `specs/` (written when P5 starts);
    - example apps `examples/` (P6);
    - docs `apps/docs/` (P7).

    Proposal files for non-verifiable phases go in `workflow/plans/flow/references/proposals/`.

    ## Stages and stage exits

    | Stage | Phases | Stage exit |
    | --- | --- | --- |
    | A Settle | P0, P1, P2 | The design and the prototype agree; Plans and core Specs written from them |
    | B Suite and core | P3, P4, P11 to P15 | The suite audited and green on both runtimes; the core passes it alone |
    | C Features | P5 | Each feature's slice audited, then green on production |
    | D Examples and docs | P6, P7 | Example apps run in real Chrome and Safari; docs generated from source |
    | E Hardening and release | P8, P9, P10 | Budgets met, findings swept, released as far as the user asks |

    ## Scope

    Bounds: P0 to P10, with P11 to P15 as the P3 and P4 area phases; UI is React and Next.js only.
    Assumptions: the prototype is the reference implementation.

    Order of authority:

    - Specs, then Plans;
    - where a Plan and the prototype disagree, the prototype wins until a Spec records it (amendment
      11);
    - features are built from the Plans so corrected.

    Specs are guides, written just in time and revised with a logged reason (amendment 13).

    Exclusions:

    - Electron;
    - TanStack DB integration;
    - tla-ts;
    - new prototyping;
    - feature Specs before P5;
    - migration functions and manual versions (revisit.md).

    Reused evidence: P0 to P2's results (stage A close-out PASS after its re-check; phase 2
    completion audit PASS-WITH-FINDINGS, fixed at `9c3fc40c`); P3 rounds 1 to 5 (suite 61/61 on
    round 14, null 0/61, falsify 25/25, `nub run check` at zero, at `bc8dffba`).

    Absorbed into the Plans and cited, not carried: D1 to D7 of the round-14 review, the required
    services list (@plan.Operations, @plan.Runtime.V2), and OD1 to OD55 through the phase 2 merge
    (the design file, `machine-design.md`, is deleted; read it in git at `20986677`).

    Agent decisions:

    - the Decision notes in log.task are agents' calls, each with its reason and how to reverse it;
    - they block no work, and P4 starts without waiting for them;
    - one the user overrules becomes rework, noted then.

    ## Constraints

    - No new prototyping; production may converge again (session, 2026-11-04; whole run).
    - The suite and the core are written cleanly, with the prototype open beside them; prototype
      code never moves into the package (session, 2026-11-04; P3, P4, P11 to P15).
    - Production code is built from `.spec` files written with skill:to-design and skill:to-spec;
      briefs cite the Spec, reviews check code against it (the user, 2026-11-09; whole run).
    - Specs are guides, written just in time, revised whenever building shows them wrong, with the
      reason logged (amendment 13, 2026-11-09; whole run).
    - Production features are built test-first (memory, 2026-11-04; P3 to P15).
    - Test the spec, not the code: expected results come from a cited Spec rule, are written before
      the code by someone other than the builder, and never come from running the code; recorded
      output is a change detector only (memory, 2026-11-04; whole run).
    - UI is React and Next.js only; Electron is out (session, 2026-11-04; whole run). TanStack DB
      integration is out (session, 2026-11-03; whole run).
    - The CLI keeps only `flow check`, `explain`, `graph`, `story` and `trace` (the user,
      2026-11-02; P4, P5).
    - Docs live in `apps/docs/`, written in the source in Effect's shape (summary, When to use,
      Details, Gotchas, named runnable examples, `@see`), generated to Markdown, with long guides
      beside the package; example apps linked from them (session, 2026-11-04; P7).
    - Example apps go in `examples/`, built with Next.js; each explains what it demos with a
      write-up and tooltips; one demos Story presentation (session, 2026-11-04; P6).
    - Code style: every brief carries AGENTS.md "Code style (v3 production)", lint-bomb's
      `tools/lint-bomb/STYLE.md` with `examples/style.ts`, `booking.ts`, `tracking.ts`, and
      `tools/lint-bomb/FLOW-STATE.md`; the core in `src/` is plain, pure, synchronous and
      deterministic, returning work as data; Effect only in `src/host/` and the edges; doc comments
      only on exported API; `nub run check:fix`, then `nub run check` at zero errors and warnings;
      the pinned `nub` toolchain (AGENTS.md; the user, 2026-11-09; P3 to P15).
    - No plan or invariant ids in code; the Spec owns them and tests cite them (session, 2026-11-04;
      whole run).
    - Conformance code stays plain synchronous values, not Context.Services (the user, 2026-11-09;
      P3).
    - Lint covers `src` and `conformance` (the user, 2026-11-09; whole run).
    - Ask in numbered rounds with before and after and a recommendation; never present a
      recommendation as decided; call him "the user" (AGENTS.md; memory; whole run).
    - Work serially: one lead at a time, reviews on events, a close-out per phase or area
      (amendment 3, 2026-11-08; whole run). Up to 8 deterministic processes, about 16 GB; real-browser runs and
      benchmarks alone; kill only this run's processes, by exact pid (memory, 2026-11-04; whole
      run).
    - Business keys for money (`prototypes/round-8/RESULTS.md`, 2026-11-01; P4, P5). Entities are
      optional but not up for cutting (memory, 2026-11-01; P5). No tla-ts (memory, 2026-11-03; whole
      run).

    ## Authority

    By default: read, edit within the phase's scope, run checks, repair own defects, update records.
    Also allowed, by the user's approval of this Epic:

    - commit locally to `flow-rewrite` with explicit paths; never push, never stash (the user's
      approval of this Epic is the "when I say" of AGENTS.md's "commit only when I say");
    - start agents as the loop's instructions say: serially, one building lead and, at review time,
      one reviewing or checking agent (a reviewing lead, or an independent skill:check-spec or
      skill:check-task agent), each lead with its own sidecars;
    - insert Tasks, split a phase that keeps its goal, reorder without breaking a dependency;
    - start the next phase after its completion audit passes, unless it starts with a stop;
    - write and revise Specs (skill:to-spec, skill:to-design), each change logged. Writing rulings
      or coverage records into the Plans outside a merge phase: list the change and wait for the
      user's yes (AGENTS.md). Orchestrator decides: design calls in P3 to P5 and P9, the suite's
      layout and the internal module layout, design calls inside one app's slice (P6) or one docs
      section (P7), judged by a reviewing agent, and small public API changes (a flag, a default, a
      field or member name, where an option sits); each noted for the user's read (amendment 9).
      User decides: public entry-point names; new concepts; large public API changes (a change to
      how app code is structured, removing a feature, anything touching many call sites); a
      guarantee change; relaxing a gate; changes to the Plans' decisions or outcomes; scope;
      the frame (skill:build-a-loop#rules, R16); which example apps, and accepting each
      app; the CLI outputs; the docs criteria; budget targets; each ship stage; more agents than the
      instructions allow. Everything else still needs the user.

    ## Limits

    No budget of iterations, time or usage, for the Epic, a phase or a Task (the user, 2026-11-10):
    the reviews and each phase's exit criteria judge progress. Failure limit (the default for each
    phase): 2 failures with the same root cause after convergence. Usage reserve: the run stops at
    75 percent weekly usage, all models (the user, 2026-11-11). Usage limits and reserves always mean
    the weekly window, never the 5-hour one; read it from the harness's usage view (in the Claude
    desktop app, its usage tool; otherwise ask the user). The user changes the reserve as planned
    work changes: a new value is an edit here and a note, with no confirmation round. R20 (b) and
    R21 at the pinned revision name budgets: this Epic has none to spend, and the reserve is its
    only usage stop.

    ## Glossary

    Library terms: skill:to-epic#terms. This project's terms:

    - Prototype runtime, round 14: `packages/flow-state-v3/prototypes/round-14/`, the reference.
    - Old suite: the prototype's `prototypes/conformance/`; the new suite is
      `packages/flow-state-v3/conformance/`.
    - Port: the new suite's one interface to a runtime, with a prototype, a null and (in P4) a
      production adapter. Oracle: a module that judges one guarantee on every step.
    - Lie: the old name for a mutant of a runtime; renamed "mutant" by P3's rename Task.
    - Falsify: the gate that runs every check against null, broken, tampered and lying runtimes.
    - Null runtime: a runtime that does nothing; it must fail every case.
    - Guarantee map: the suite README's table of each Spec Invariant, its oracle and its mutants.
    - Graph gate: `flow graph` checked against every transition the runtime is seen to take.
    - Fast gates and final-commit gates: gates run on every commit, and the slower ones (full mutant
      run, searches, real-browser runs) run before a phase's final commit.
    - The Pay path: checkout's Pay machine run end to end through the public API.
    - Safari's four requirements: host adapter requirements in
      `packages/flow-state-v3/prototypes/safari/RESULTS.md`.
    - Issue catalog: every issue the runtime raises, with a name, cause and next step, generated
      from the code.
    - Packed-consumer check: installing the exact published tarball in a fresh project and importing
      every public route.
    - Carry list: round-14 items owned by the new suite, in its README.
    - OD, C, D, U ids: decision ids of `machine-design.md` (deleted; in git at `20986677`) and its
      convergence records.
    - Amendment N: a numbered change to the old roadmap (git `b30be69d`); each one that still
      applies has an Amendment note in log.task, which names its number and says what it changed.
skill        workflow@e74b0e2
run
    To start: "run the loop in workflow/tasks/V3/epic.task". You are the orchestrator: you start,
    track and route agents; you never build.

    1. Read this file, `workflow show @epic.V3 --tree`, and the records the current Task links; on a
       resume, run skill:catch-up first. If status is not active or the loop fields are missing,
       stop and tell the user.
    2. Current Task: the in_progress one, otherwise `workflow next --epic V3`. It returns "No ready
       tasks." while the Epic is draft (readiness needs an active Epic) and while P4 to P15's Tasks
       are draft: set a phase's Tasks open when the phase starts.
    3. Create the session cron from cadence; without one, run its prompt at each wake (R25).
    4. Keep the machine awake for the run (R26), for example `caffeinate -i` on this Mac.
    5. Recover the real agent state: agents and processes alive, branch, last checkpoint, and
       uncommitted work with its owner.
    6. Note "Run started" or "Run resumed": revision, current Task, live agents, first action.
    7. Start or resume one building lead, set its Task in_progress, and confirm activity within a
       minute. Rules: follow skill:build-a-loop#rules, skill:policy-delegation and
       skill:policy-verification at the library revision in skill; instructions override their
       defaults. The skills root is the suite path `suites/workflow` in the library checkout
       `/Users/arpit/Developer/workflow/skills` (a Git repository); check the pin with
       `git -C /Users/arpit/Developer/workflow/skills rev-parse --short HEAD`. If it is at another
       revision, read the pinned one with
       `git -C /Users/arpit/Developer/workflow/skills show e74b0e2:suites/workflow/NAME/SKILL.md`,
       or ask the user. Before changing the loop fields (run, instructions, cadence, stops), read
       skill:build-a-loop at the pinned revision; before adding or editing a Task, read
       skill:to-task (and skill:to-epic for a phase); before adding a phase, read skill:to-epic.
instructions
    **Who runs the loop**

    - Every public feature in the Plans is represented in fixtures, and the same fixtures feed both the
      conformance suite (judged by oracles) and the benchmark suite (replayed without oracles); a checked
      feature matrix maps each Plan feature id to its fixtures, and a gate fails on a feature with no fixture
      (the user, 2026-11-11).
    - An agent orchestrator (Claude) until a stop (roadmap, 2026-11-07). It never builds: it starts and tracks leads,
      verifies their findings, and sends the accepted ones to the building lead in the same wake.
    - One lead at a time: the building lead, and at review time one reviewing or checking agent (a reviewing lead, or
      an independent skill:check-spec or skill:check-task agent) in the review slot. Design, Spec and audit leads
      take the building lead's slot in turn (amendment 3; the user, 2026-11-11, to preserve usage).
    - One builder carries related Tasks; a fresh one starts only when context runs long or work moves from suite to
      production. Leads work through up to three context compactions before handing off (the user, 2026-11-11).
    - Leads report once per batch or stop, in at most 10 lines, no progress messages: verdict, commits, gate counts,
      and the items needing a decision. Full findings and sidecar logs go in the Task's comments, STATUS.md or a
      review file, and the report names where (the user, 2026-11-11). Sidecars report only to their lead.
    - Source of truth: the core Specs for the core; the Plans for the rest; where a Plan and the prototype disagree,
      the prototype, until a Spec records it (amendment 11; holds until P5's feature Specs exist).
    - P3 and P4 interleave by area (the user, 2026-11-11): T16, T17, T18, then T10 and T19, T11 and T20, T9 and T12
      and T21, T22, then T15 as the final cross-area audit before T23. An area's suite is green, null-red,
      mutant-proven, reviewed and adversarially audited (its own auditor, its own mutants) before its production
      module starts; when the two disagree the Spec decides and the fix cites its rule; a case is never edited just
      to pass.
    - Checkpoint: a builder commit at the end of a coherent step, about every 50 minutes.

    **Agents and effort**

    - Opus leads and Haiku sidecars, Claude Code (detected 2026-11-09). Every lead (building, reviewing, auditing,
      designing) runs at medium effort; every Haiku agent at high, and a lead tells its own sidecars so (the user,
      2026-11-11).
    - Building leads plan the round up front and write every committed line; Haiku never writes committed code (the
      user, 2026-11-10). Up to four Haiku sidecars as runners (gates, falsify, sweeps, traces), one-rule diff
      reviewers, locators (prototype code, old case and old mutant per Invariant as file:line lists), and writers of
      adversarial mutants from the Spec in scratch (the lead rewrites any it keeps). A reviewer sidecar checks every
      oracle, test harness or runtime change (amendment 8).
    - A lead reads every line a sidecar wrote, re-runs each check itself, confirms a mutant fails for the expected
      reason, and logs each result as accepted, changed or rejected in the Task's comments, and in STATUS.md for P3
      (the user, 2026-11-08).
    - Processes: up to 8 deterministic ones, about 16 GB, after checking `vm_stat`; browser runs and benchmarks alone.

    **Building**

    - Every build and review brief carries the Constraints' code-style list, the Spec it implements and the
      test-the-spec rule, and tells the lead to run `workflow brief @task.V3.Tn` and read its phase's skills first
      (the user, 2026-11-09 and 2026-11-10). Name a skill once, on the phase.
    - Plan changes found while building go to @plan.Pending as decisions, never into the Plans; one merges only once
      building proves it right and the user says yes (the user, 2026-11-11).
    - Effect prior art (the user, 2026-11-11): each module's Spec skeleton (skill:to-design) lists the Effect features
      that cover its needs and why each is used or skipped; a reviewer checks it, and what a change re-creates of
      Effect is a standing review angle. Lint bans `setTimeout`, `clearTimeout`, `Date.now`, `performance.now` and
      `Math.random` in `src/` outside the real host's adapter.
    - Adapters use typed failures, defects apart; from P4, a throw outside a defect in `src/` is a standing review
      angle (the user, 2026-11-11). Production is driven through its public API (`src/index.ts`) plus a short listed
      seam (advisor answers, adopted 2026-11-11, in the proposals folder's advisor-answers-2026-11-11.md).

    **Testing and the suite**

    - Test first, at the module boundary (the user, 2026-11-11; skill:policy-testing in `.agents/skills`): before
      code, write module integration tests through the public interface with real components, not unit tests of
      internals, and the cases that enforce each Invariant the work implements; then build until they pass.
    - Expected results come from an executable Spec model that computes them and generates cases; a different model
      family reads a sample (T45). Haiku no longer drafts them. Obligations come only from suite facts, enforced by
      type and lint (T44). Strength is mutation score, liar score, oracle share and table-cell reach, not Invariant
      count; round-14 green is no longer an exit, a mutant host with a verified limits list is.
    - Every suite brief and review applies skill:policy-testing#audit-a-suite as a checklist: each check's inputs
      inventoried (observed by the harness or reported by the runtime), excuses only from observed causes, a mutant
      for each report a check reads, expected results never copied from round-14 (the user, 2026-11-11).
    - A coverage row leaves P3 only with evidence: the round-14 and old-suite code searched, and why it cannot show
      the Invariant; the next review checks each claim (the orchestrator, 2026-11-11).
    - Convergence threshold 3: the third review in a row finding one root cause in one part (amendment 4, the user,
      2026-11-08); only when rounds keep surfacing many issues, otherwise fix the known bugs and go on (the user,
      2026-11-10). No per-step reviews inside a convergence: its acceptance is the class gate.

    **Reviews**

    - Reviews start on events, not the clock: a lost catch in the catch matrix, a Port change, a new oracle, a Spec
      change, or an area's gate. Machine gates run on every checkpoint. Reviews per Task were declined. Loop
      amendments are batched at an area's close-out unless one fixes a wrong result (the user, 2026-11-11).
    - Every review, audit or research task goes to one Opus lead with zero to two read-only Haiku sidecars by need;
      code quality is left to lint. A close-out review ends each phase or area.

    **Records, usage, commands**

    - Records lead with the bottom line and hold each fact once (the user, 2026-11-11): an Epic note is 1 to 3 lines
      with one Why and one Reverse for a decision; a Task comment is a verdict line plus a link to its STATUS.md
      section or review file; sidecar logs and findings tables live only there; STATUS.md keeps one short section per
      Task, cut to a summary when done; no commit lists or gate counts beyond the one revision verified. Briefs and
      handoffs follow the user's Writing order.
    - Usage: the run stops at the reserve in Limits and at nothing else; no budget of iterations, time or usage.
    - Commands: under 5 minutes each, with `perl -e 'alarm N; exec @ARGV' --` or `watch.ts --seconds N`; stdin never
      read (`</dev/null`); files only with the Write and Edit tools (memory, 2026-11-04).
    - R43: no worktrees; the builder works in the main checkout on `flow-rewrite` (the user, 2026-11-08).
cadence
    Reviews start on events (Reviews, in instructions), not on a clock; machine gates run on every checkpoint. A
    fallback wake every 3 hours checks only for a stalled lead and the usage reserve.

    Session cron `23 */3 * * *` (the stall check).

    Prompt: "V3 review wake. Read workflow/tasks/V3/epic.task and the last notes in its log.task. If its Limits
    section sets a usage reserve, read the weekly usage from the harness's usage view, and if the reserve is reached,
    stop the run by R21 and note it. If a review event has happened with no review or audit covering it, start one
    Opus reviewing lead at medium effort with sidecars by need; verify its findings, send the accepted ones to the
    building lead in the same wake, and note the review. If the building lead has shown no activity for 70 minutes,
    resume it by message or replace it (R7) and note it. Otherwise write nothing." Quiet rule: a wake that finds
    nothing writes no note and sends no message.
stops
    - The user's decisions in the Authority section;
    - a guarantee that can't be met;
    - a guarantee change;
    - a change proposal whose judge is the user;
    - the user-judged decisions in P5, P6, P7 and P9 (their cards);
    - each ship stage in P10;
    - the real-phone run in P9, which holds only that item.
